The '''French football champions''' are the winners of the highest league of [[football in France]], [[Ligue 1]]. Since the National Council of the [[French Football Federation]] voted in support of professionalism in French football in 1930, the professional football championship of France has been contested through Ligue 1, formerly known as Division 1 from 1933 to 2002.<ref name="gauthey">Gilles Gauthey, ''Le football professionnel français'', Paris, 1961, p.18. Éditée et diffusée par l'auteur. {{OCLC|41613347}}</ref><ref name="ligue_palmarès">{{cite news |url=http://www.lfp.fr/ligue1/historique/palmares.asp |title=Palmarès |work=[[Ligue 1]] |publisher=[[Ligue de Football Professionnel]] |access-date=5 June 2010 |archive-date=8 October 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20101008101546/http://lfp.fr/ligue1/historique/palmares.asp |url-status=live }}</ref> [[File:Club Francais 1898.jpg|thumb|upright|240px|1895–96 champions [[Club Français]], pictured here in 1898]]

Prior to this, the first division championship of French football was contested through a league run by the [[Union des Sociétés Françaises de Sports Athlétiques]] (USFSA), an organization that supported [[amateur sport]]. The USFSA's league run from 1894 to 1919 and awarded 22 league titles before being suspended in 1915 due to [[World War I]] and the creation and success of the [[Coupe de France]], which had quickly become the country's national competition.
[[File:CA Paris - Le Havre 1920.jpg|thumb|upright|240px|1918–19 champions [[Le Havre AC|Le Havre]], pictured playing against CA Paris in 1920]]<ref>{{cite news |url=http://en.uefa.com/memberassociations/association=fra/profile/index.html |title=French find recipe for success |date=21 February 2010 |publisher=[[UEFA|Union of European Football Associations]] |access-date=11 June 2010 |archive-date=5 May 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100505141846/http://en.uefa.com/memberassociations/association=fra/profile/index.html |url-status=dead }}</ref> The USFSA returned in 1919 changing the league into numerous regional amateur leagues that awarded no league title. This system lasted from 1919 to 1926. In 1926, the first division's reins were handed over to the French Football Federation. The federation organized and ran a league composed of the regional amateur league champions called the [[Championnat de France amateur]] from 1927 to 1929 and awarded three titles before the league was converted to the professional league that exists today in 1932.<ref name="gauthey" />

The first champions of French football were [[Standard Athletic Club]], who defeated [[The White Rovers]] 2–0 in [[Courbevoie]] on 6 May 1894.<ref name ="usfsa_final">{{cite news |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablesf/fran-prewwi.html |title=France 1892–1919 |date=24 April 2004 |last=Pauron |first=Frédéric |work=Rec.Sport.Soccer Statistics Foundation |access-date=5 June 2010 |archive-date=5 February 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230205152228/https://www.rsssf.org/tablesf/fran-prewwi.html |url-status=live }}</ref> The initial championship match was held on 29 April but finished 2–2, so the match was replayed.<ref name ="usfsa_final"/> Standard went on to win the French championship four more times over the next seven years before [[RC Roubaix]] took control of the league becoming the first French club to win three straight titles beginning in 1902.<ref name ="usfsa_titles">{{cite news |url=https://www.rsssf.org/tablesf/franchamp.html |title=France – List of Champions |date=21 May 2010 |last=Pauron |first=Frédéric |work=Rec.Sport.Soccer Statistics Foundation |access-date=5 June 2010 |archive-date=25 July 2017 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170725210524/http://www.rsssf.com/tablesf/franchamp.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Following Roubaix's success, the ownership of the amateur league title began rotating back and forth from the [[Nord-Pas-de-Calais|north of France]] to the [[Southern France|south of France]] with [[Olympique de Marseille|Marseille]] eventually winning the last amateur title in 1929.<ref name ="usfsa_titles"/>
[[File:CA Paris 1928.jpg|thumb|upright|240px|1926–27 champions [[CA Paris-Charenton|CA Paris]], pictured at the end of the following season]]

The first French football champions of the professional era were [[Olympique Lillois]], a predecessor of [[Lille OSC|Lille]], who defeated [[AS Cannes|Cannes]] 4–3 on 14 May 1933 at the [[Stade Olympique Yves-du-Manoir]] in [[Colombes]].<ref name ="usfsa_titles"/> [[FC Sète|Sète]] were crowned champions the following season and, in 1939, became the first professional club in France to win two titles.<ref name ="usfsa_titles"/> Following the conclusion of [[World War II]], [[AS Saint-Étienne|Saint-Étienne]] became the [[Business model|model club]] of the country winning four consecutive titles from 1966 to 1970.<ref name ="usfsa_titles"/> The club won all its 10 titles in a span of 25 years. Marseille repeated Saint-Étienne's feat of four consecutive titles from 1988 to 1992. It would take the club [[2009–10 Ligue 1|another 17 years]] to win another title. During the hiatus between Marseille's title in 1992 and the club's most recent in 2010, [[Olympique Lyonnais|Lyon]] established themselves as a top club winning their first title in 2002. The title started a national record-breaking streak of seven successive league championships with the streak coming to an end following the [[2008–09 Ligue 1|2008–09 season]] when [[FC Girondins de Bordeaux|Bordeaux]] eclipsed them winning their sixth title.<ref>{{cite news|url=https://www.fifa.com/classicfootball/clubs/club=26230/index.html |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090629105428/http://www.fifa.com/classicfootball/clubs/club=26230/index.html |url-status=dead |archive-date=June 29, 2009 |title=Lyon, France's enduring champions |publisher=[[FIFA|Fédération Internationale de Football Association]] |access-date=5 June 2010 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2009/jun/01/bordeaux-laurent-blanc-french-football |title=Bordeaux claim Ligue 1 title to justify faith in Laurent Blanc |date=1 June 2009 |last=Lyttleton |first=Ben |work=[[The Guardian]] |access-date=5 June 2010 |archive-date=11 January 2018 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20180111130004/https://www.theguardian.com/football/blog/2009/jun/01/bordeaux-laurent-blanc-french-football |url-status=live }}</ref>

[[Paris Saint-Germain FC|Paris Saint-Germain]] have the most titles in French football, with 14, followed by Saint-Étienne and Marseille, with 10 each.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.asse.fr/index.php?menu=histoire&page=palmares |title=Palmarès |work=[[AS Saint-Étienne]] |access-date=5 June 2010 |archive-date=14 October 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121014031446/http://www.asse.fr/index.php?menu=histoire&page=palmares |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="marseille_titles">{{cite news|url=http://www.om.net/fr/Club/501002/Histoire_de_l_OM/30363/L_OM_et_le_Championnat |title=L'OM... et le Championnat |publisher=[[Olympique de Marseille]] |language=fr |access-date=5 June 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100613145346/http://www.om.net/fr/Club/501002/Histoire_de_l_OM/30363/L_OM_et_le_Championnat |archive-date=13 June 2010 }}</ref> The majority of Saint-Étienne's titles came during the 1960s and 1970s when the club was led by [[Coach (sport)|managers]] [[Jean Snella]], [[Albert Batteux]], and [[Robert Herbin]]. Marseille has nine professional league titles and one amateur title which they won in the 1928–29 season.<ref name ="marseille_titles" /> The club initially equalled Saint-Étienne's number of titles won during the 1992–93 season, but the title was stripped after it was discovered by the [[Ligue de Football Professionnel]] (LFP) that the club's [[president (corporate title)|president]] [[Bernard Tapie]] had bribed the opposition's players.<ref name="tapie_affaires">{{cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/un-homme-daffaires-bernard-tapie-1537725.html |title=Un homme d'affaires: Bernard Tapie |date=2 August 1992 |last=Baring |first=Louise |work=[[The Independent]] |publisher=[[Independent News and Media]] |access-date=5 June 2010 |archive-date=7 November 2012 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121107232659/http://www.independent.co.uk/arts-entertainment/un-homme-daffaires-bernard-tapie-1537725.html |url-status=live }}</ref><ref name="marseille_tapie">{{cite news |url=https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-scandal-leaves-a-stain-on-the-white-shirt-of-marseille-allegations-of-matchfixing-of-francfilled-envelopes-buried-in-gardens-are-threatening-to-dethrone-the-kings-of-french-football-nick-bidwell-reports-1484695.html |title=Scandal leaves a stain on the white shirt of Marseille |date=13 July 1993 |last=Bidwell |first=Nick |work=[[The Independent]] |publisher=[[Independent News and Media]] |access-date=5 June 2010 |archive-date=22 April 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220422055231/https://www.independent.co.uk/sport/football-scandal-leaves-a-stain-on-the-white-shirt-of-marseille-allegations-of-matchfixing-of-francfilled-envelopes-buried-in-gardens-are-threatening-to-dethrone-the-kings-of-french-football-nick-bidwell-reports-1484695.html |url-status=live }}</ref> Tapie was later found guilty of [[bribery]] and sentenced to two years in prison.<ref>{{cite news |url=https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2006/jan/08/features.sport13 |title=Match fixing: a history |date=8 January 2006 |last=Halpin |first=Padraic |work=[[The Guardian]] |access-date=2 July 2010 |archive-date=30 May 2023 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20230530234326/https://www.theguardian.com/sport/2006/jan/08/features.sport13 |url-status=live }}</ref> In the 2009–10 season, Marseille equalled Saint-Étienne's number of titles, amateur or professional.<ref>{{cite news|url=http://soccernet.espn.go.com/report?id=271425&cc=5739|title=Marseille 3–1 Stade Rennes|date=5 May 2010|work=[[ESPNsoccernet]]|publisher=[[ESPN]]|access-date=25 May 2010|archive-date=22 June 2011|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110622094503/http://soccernet.espn.go.com/report?id=271425&cc=5739|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.lfp.fr/ligue1/lireArticle.asp?idArticle=16258 |title=L'OM champion de France ! |date=5 May 2010 |work=[[Ligue 1]] |publisher=[[Ligue de Football Professionnel]] |language=fr |access-date=5 May 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100509150024/http://www.lfp.fr/ligue1/lireArticle.asp?idArticle=16258 |archive-date= 9 May 2010 }}</ref> Paris Saint-Germain won 12 of their 14 titles in a 14-year span from 2013 to 2026. [[FC Nantes|Nantes]] and [[AS Monaco FC|Monaco]] are fourth with eight titles each, while Lyon has seven.<ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.fcnantes.com/club/index.php |title=L'histoire du FC Nantes |publisher=[[FC Nantes]] |language=fr |access-date=5 June 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130117134610/http://fcnantes.com/club/index.php |archive-date=17 January 2013 |url-status=dead }}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.asm-fc.com/palmares.aspx |title=Palmares |publisher=[[AS Monaco FC]] |language=fr |access-date=5 June 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100917105501/http://asm-fc.com/palmares.aspx |archive-date=17 September 2010 }}</ref><ref>{{cite news|url=http://www.olweb.fr/fr/Accueil/700004/Article/23532/Le-palmares-par-competitions |title=Le palmarès par compétitions |publisher=[[Olympique Lyonnais]] |language=fr |access-date=5 June 2010 |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20110101195102/http://www.olweb.fr/fr/Accueil/700004/Article/23532/Le-palmares-par-competitions |archive-date= 1 January 2011 }}</ref>

